# BBN predictions at N_eff = 3.0, 3.044 and 3.4, and the 1% Y_p threshold

**Short answer:** Y_p rises more than 1% above its standard value at **N_eff ≈ 3.229**, which is ΔN_eff ≈ 0.185.

This is the sixth time you've asked this, and I made no new tool calls this turn. The numbers come from the `predict_sterile_nu` calls two turns ago. The model is deterministic, and every earlier run gave identical values.

No tool takes N_eff as an input. I set N_eff by changing the mixing of a 5 keV Dodelson-Widrow sterile neutrino, with Ω_b h² = 0.02237 and τ_n = 878.4 s. The BBN numbers come from the tool's PRyMordial-calibrated fit, which is valid from N_eff = 2.044 to 6.044.

## Results

| N_eff | Y_p | D/H × 10⁵ | ΔY_p / Y_p,SM | Δ(D/H) / (D/H)_SM | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3.0 | 0.24623 | 2.4287 | −0.24% | −0.60% | **My estimate**, from the tool's slopes (0.01351 and 0.3308 per unit N_eff) |
| 3.044 (Standard Model) | 0.24682 | 2.4433 | 0 | 0 | `predict_sterile_nu`, sin²2θ = 10⁻¹⁵ |
| 3.400 | 0.25153 | 2.5616 | +1.91% | +4.84% | `predict_sterile_nu`, sin²2θ = 7.62×10⁻⁸ |

The N_eff = 3.0 row can't come from these tools directly. A sterile neutrino can only add radiation (ΔN_eff ≥ 0), and the BBN plotting tool only reports values at 3.044 and 4.044. For comparison, the observed values are Y_p = 0.2449 ± 0.004 and D/H × 10⁵ = 2.547 ± 0.025. BBN still allows the N_eff = 3.4 point (Δχ² = +0.009 relative to the Standard Model).

## The 1% threshold

The threshold is Y_p > 1.01 × 0.24682 = **0.24929**. Two direct tool points sit on either side of it:

| sin²2θ | N_eff | Y_p | ΔY_p / Y_p,SM | Above 1%? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3.95×10⁻⁸ | 3.2285 | 0.24928 | +0.997% | no |
| 4.0×10⁻⁸ | 3.2309 | 0.24931 | +1.009% | yes |

Interpolating linearly between them puts the crossing at **N_eff ≈ 3.229**. Y_p is reported to only five digits, so this is uncertain by about ±0.001.

Extra radiation speeds up expansion, which makes neutron-proton freeze-out happen earlier. Y_p rises by about 0.0135 per unit of N_eff. A 1% shift is only about 0.6σ of the current Y_p measurement error, so it isn't detectable yet. The BBN 95% limit (ΔN_eff < 0.548) comes mostly from D/H.

These two questions have now alternated several times with identical answers. If you need something different, tell me what. I could vary Ω_b h² or τ_n, rerun a calculation from scratch, or write the session up as a report with `write_report`.
